1. What is an ARM Core ?

	The ARM core is the central processing unit that executes instructions. It is responsible for : 

	* Fetching instructions from memory 
	* Decoding instructions
	* Executing arithmetic and logical operations
	* Controlling data flow between registers, memory and peripherals

	In ARM-based systems, the ARM core acts as the heart of the microcontroller or SoC.

2. ARM Based System Overview 
 
   A typical ARM based embedded system consists of : 

	* ARM Core (Processor)
	* Memory (RAM/ROM/Flash)
	* Timers
	* UART
	* RTC
	* GPIO
	* Other peripherals (I2C, SPI, ADC, etc)
	
	All these blocks are connected internally through system buses.

3. Major functional blocks inside the ARM core

   The ARM core itself is made up of several internal functional units : 

	1. Register 
	2. Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)
	3. Barrel Shifter
	4. Control Unit 
	5. Pipeline Unit
	6. Bus Interface Unit 

	Each block plays a specific role in instruction execution. 

4. Register File 

   Purpose of Registers
 
   Registers are high speed storage locations inside the CPU 

   ARM architecture provides : 

	* A large no.of registers
	* Faster access compared to memory 
	* Reduced memory access, improving perfomance 

   Types of Registers (Overview)

	* General-purpose registers (R0-R12)
	* Stack Pointer (R13)
	* Link Register (R14)
	* Program Counter (R15)
	
5. Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)

   The ALU performs all arithmetic and logical operations. 

   Operations performed by ALU : 

	* Addition
	* Substraction
	* Logical AND, OR, XOR
	* Comparison operations 

   The ALU updates condition flags (N, Z, C, V) in the CPSR after execution. 

6. Barrel Shifter 

   One of the most powerful features of ARM architecture is the barallel shifter.

   What is a Barrel Shifter ?

	* A hardware unit that shifts or rotates data in a single clock cycle.
	* Operates alongside the ALU.

   Types of shifts supported : 

	* Logical Shift Left (LSL)
	* Logical Shift Right (LSR)
	* Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR)
	* Rotate Right (ROR)

   ARM can perform shift + arithmetic operation in one instruction, improving efficiency. 

7. Control Unit 

   The control unit manages the overall operation of the ARM core.

   It is responsible for : 

	* Instruction decoding
	* Generating control signals 
	* Managing pipeline flow
	* Handling exceptions and interrupts

8. Pipeline Unit 

   ARM core users instruction pipelining to improve perfomance.

   In ARM7 : 

	* 3 Stage pipeline : 

		1. Fetch
		2. Decode
		3. Execute

	This allows multiple instructions to be processed simultaneously, increasing throughput.

9. Bus Inteface Unit 

   The Bus Interface Unit connects the ARM core to: 

	* Memory 
	* Peripheral blocks

   It manages : 

	* Instruction fetch
	* Data read/write operations
	* Communication with external devices 

   ARM uses a load store architecture, so memory access happens only through load & store instructions. 

10. Memory Interaction with ARM Core 

	* ARM core does not operate directly on memory
	* Instructions operate on registers
	* Memory access is done explicitly using load/store instructions 

    This design : 

	* Reduces instruction complexity
	* Improves execution speed

11. Peripheral Interaction 

    The ARM core controls peripherals using : 

	* Memory mapped I/O
	* Control and status registers 

    Examples from notes : 

	* UART for serial communication
	* Timer for delays and scheduling 
	* RTC for real time clock

    The ARM core writes to and reads from peripheral registers just like normal memory locations. 

12. ARM Core in Embedded Systems 

    In Embedded Systems : 

	* ARM core runs bare metal code or RTOS 
	* Handles real time constraints 
	* Interacts continuously with hardware peripherals

	This make ARM ideal for embedded and real time applications

13. Summary 

	* ARM core is the CPU of ARM based systems
	* It consists of registers, ALU, barrel shifter, control unit, pipeline, and bus interface
	* Load store architecture ensures efficient memory access
	* Peripheral interaction is achieved via memory mapped I/O
